+# A dated FAA investigation supplement and congressional production trail
+
+Public derivative of the November 10, 2026 source review · B129 / PD02–PD04 procedural comparison. The House record identifies the FAA internal **Report of Investigation ASW20070070**, describes separate closure, reopening and supplement dates, and cites the congressional letter used to obtain it. A separately dated FAA management response is preserved inside an OIG audit. These records concern the Southwest Airlines airworthiness oversight inquiry. The case-specific outcomes of the earlier September 11 Commission referral **CC-2006-085 / 042R0122001** remain open. [House source, PDF pp. 9–10](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-110hhrg41821/pdf/CHRG-110hhrg41821.pdf#page=9) · [September 11 referral, p. 12](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12)
+
+## Separate stages recorded by the sources
+
+| Source | Recorded observation |
+| --- | --- |
+| **House hearing committee material**, PDF p. 9, printed vii, n. 5 and p. 10, printed viii, nn. 12–13 | Identifies FAA file `ASW20070070`. The footnote on p. 10 says the internal investigation began in **May 2007**, closed in **July 2007**, reopened on **October 2, 2007** and acquired a supplement on **October 5, 2007**. The body reports that majority staff obtained the investigation after the two committee chairs threatened a subpoena in an **October 5, 2007** letter to the Acting FAA Administrator. The supplement and the congressional request share a reported date. The record here does not establish the time of either, the order of events, or what the production contained. [Committee record](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-110hhrg41821/pdf/CHRG-110hhrg41821.pdf#page=10) |
+| **April 3, 2008 OIG written testimony**, PDF pp. 19, 30–31 | The OIG dates the regional request to Security and Hazardous Materials to **May 1, 2007**, the division's initial report to **July 12**, and the request for a second review to **September 18**. Its timeline places the **second review report** on **October 2**. Preserve this beside the House account of **reopening October 2** and **adding a supplement October 5**. The versions and routing record would show whether the terms describe different documents, different events, or different dates for the same event. The timeline separately gives **November 16, 2007** for initiating carrier enforcement. [OIG text](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/OIG_STATEMENT_ON_SWA.pdf#page=19) · [Timeline](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/OIG_STATEMENT_ON_SWA.pdf#page=30) |
+| **OIG and FAA April 3 accounts of the inspector's status** | OIG's written testimony says he was “never completely relieved of his duties” and says he was transferred. The FAA official says the inspector had “been removed from aviation safety inspector duties” and was subject to an ongoing personnel action. Preserve both descriptions. Effective dates, duty assignments and decision records would establish whether they refer to different intervals or different duties. Neither passage supplies a signed personnel outcome. [OIG testimony, PDF p. 20 lines 24–30](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/OIG_STATEMENT_ON_SWA.pdf#page=20) · [House transcript, PDF p. 90 lines 11–15, printed 66](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-110hhrg41821/pdf/CHRG-110hhrg41821.pdf#page=90) |
+| **June 30 OIG audit AV-2008-057 and reproduced June 24 FAA response** | Exhibit A says auditors reviewed the division's report and interviewed the investigator. The FAA CFO's June 24 memo records **Non-Concur** on audit recommendation **3** and **Partially Concur** on **8**. For 8 it cites the Safety Issues Report System and records **24** potential issues, **18** emailed directly and **6** entered into the automated system. It separately identifies **11** as appropriate to accept in SIRS. OIG says SIRS does not meet the recommendation's intent. Its Actions Required section gives **15 calendar days** for a revised response to 3 and 8, and separately describes 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 7 as resolved pending planned action. The audit's appendix is a copy of the management response. The revised response and final implementation record remain unacquired. [Methods, PDF p. 14](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/SWA_Report_Issued.pdf#page=14) · [FAA response, pp. 16–18](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/SWA_Report_Issued.pdf#page=16) · [OIG evaluation and action, pp. 11–13](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/SWA_Report_Issued.pdf#page=11) |
+
+The SIRS arithmetic confirms that 18 plus 6 accounts for the 24 reported submissions. The 11 is the memo's separate acceptance count. The memo does not supply the submission-level ledger or its distribution across the two intake routes. The audit responses and its explicit 15-day deadline belong to **AV-2008-057**. A shared department and nearby dates leave the policy, response clock and routing for **CC-2006-085** unverified.
+
+## Verification and open inputs
+
+The full 744-page House PDF was read in memory and hashed. An embedded-text screen found no exact ASW20070070 match. Targeted OCR and visual review found the printed case number and the October dates on physical pp. 9–10. The official HTML includes testimony but lacks that exact file number in its saved text. Preserve each representation and its scope. A prior capped attempt obtained no House PDF body on disk. The Australian Senate candidate returned HTTP 403 HTML and was excluded from the substantive comparison. The two OIG PDFs were preserved in full. The reproducible checks cover source and derivative hashes, the selected lines, page provenance and the SIRS arithmetic. The full set of hearing exhibits was not visually inspected.
+
+- **Southwest comparator.** Seek the original ASW20070070 cover and contents, May assignment, July report and closure, September request, October 2 version, October 5 supplement and congressional letter, and the FAA dispatch and House receipt or production inventory. Retrieve the signed personnel decision separately from any reassignment or carrier enforcement. The June 24 audit response, any revised response and OIG's final closure record are distinct.
+- **PD02–PD04.** For the September 11 referral, seek the FAA investigative or administrative intake number and any cross-reference to the DOT OIG case, the file and supplement list, and parallel executive, congressional and OIG dispatch and receipt records. Search the actual management decision office and its authorized retention series. This comparator supplies those distinct record types. The join to **042R0122001 / CC-2006-085** remains open.
+- **PD01.** Keep the corrected May 2003 answer and dated receipt as separate indispensable inputs. The comparator's audit recommendations 1 and 2 are unrelated to the September 11 referral's recommendations 1 and 2. [Case-specific queue](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/055ef54a857b46846d0038109bab7e108970f03a/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-postreport-disposition-2026-10-27/OPEN-RECORDS.md)
